\pard\sb100\sa100\cf2\lang1033\b InterestPayment\cf0\b0  Routine Calculates the interest portion of a loan payment. \par
\cf3\b Syntax\cf0\b0  \par
function \b InterestPayment\b0 (const \b Rate\b0 : Extended, \b Period\b0 : Integer, \b NPeriods\b0 : Integer, const \b PresentValue\b0 : Extended, const \b FutureValue\b0 : Extended, \b PaymentTime\b0 : TPaymentTime): Extended; \par
\cf3\b Description\cf0\par
\b0  Call InterestPayment to calculate the portion of a loan payment that reflects the interest. \b Rate\b0  represents the fixed periodic interest rate. \b Period\b0  identifies the payment period. \b NPeriods\b0  is the number of periods of the loan. \b PresentValue\b0  represents the amount borrowed (the principal). \b FutureValue\b0  is the future value of the investment. \b PaymentTime\b0  indicates whether the cash flows occur at the beginning or end of the period (enter \b 0\b0  if payments are at the end of each period, \b 1\b0  if they are at the beginning). \line\pard\cf1\lang1049 =====================================================\cf0\par
\par
\pard\sb100\sa100\cf2\lang1033\b InterestRate\cf0\b0  Routine Returns the interest rate required to increase PresentValue to FutureValue.\par
\cf3\b Syntax\cf0\b0  \par
function \b InterestRate\b0 (\b NPeriods\b0 : Integer, const \b Payment\b0 : Extended, const \b PresentValue\b0 : Extended, const \b FutureValue\b0 : Extended, \b PaymentTime\b0 : TPaymentTime): Extended; \par
\cf3\b Description\cf0\b0  \par
InterestRate calculates the interest rate required in order for an investment of \b PresentValue\b0 , with periodic payments of \b Payment\b0 , to be worth \b FutureValue\b0  within \b NPeriods\b0  compounding periods. If \b NPeriods\b0  represents years, an annual interest rate results; if NPeriods represents months, a monthly interest rate results, and so on. The \b PaymentTime\b0  parameter indicates whether the cash flows occur at the beginning or end of the period (enter \b 0\b0  if payments are at the end of each period, \b 1\b0  if they are at the beginning).  \line\pard\cf1\lang1049 =====================================================\cf0\par
\par
\pard\sb100\sa100\cf2\lang1033\b InternalRateOfReturn\cf0\b0  Routine Calculates the internal rate of return for an investment. \par
\cf3\b Syntax\cf0\b0  \par
function \b InternalRateOfReturn\b0 (const \b Guess\b0 : Extended, const \b CashFlows\b0 : array of Double (\b 1,2,3...\b0 )): Extended; \par
\cf3\b Description\b0  \cf0\par
InternalRateOfReturn determines the internal rate of return on an investment. It references an array that contains cash flow information and uses the supplied internal rate of return estimate to calculate results. Before using this function, define an array containing expected cash flow amounts over a period of time. It is assumed that the amounts are received at regular intervals. Negative amounts are interpreted as cash outflows, and positive amounts as inflows. The first amount must be a negative number, to reflect the initial investment. The following amounts can all be the same for each time period, or they can be different (including a mixture of negatives, positives, or zeros). Specify the estimated rate of return as the \b Guess\b0  parameter. Pass the array of expected cash flow amounts as the \b CashFlows\b0  parameter. \line\pard\cf1\lang1049 =====================================================\cf0\par
\par
\pard\sb100\sa100\cf2\lang1033\b IsNan\cf0\b0  Routine Indicates when a variable or expression does not evaluate to a numeric value. \par
